    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0090",
      "sequence": 102,
      "meaning": "Bhavānī smiles and tells the wise sages they have spoken suitably according to their understanding: in their view Kāma has only now been burned, implying that Śambhu previously possessed passion. In hers, Śiva has always been a yogī—unborn, faultless, desireless and without sensual enjoyment. If she served him with this understanding and with love in deed, mind and speech, merciful Īśa will make her vow true. Calling Śiva the burner of Kāma is the sages’ great error: fire naturally consumes, and no snow can survive near it. In the same way, desire inevitably perishes on approaching Maheśa. The sages rejoice at her loving faith, bow to Bhavānī and go to Himālaya.",
